WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.220
Anyone who knows me
knows that I love to talk.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.420
I'm a chatterbox, just ask anyone.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.700
I like to think that I'm pretty good
at building rapport with my clients.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:19.100
And having spent just under a decade
working as a lawyer,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.140
I tell you, this is one skill
that really came in handy.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.700
Now, I remember this one
interstate client in particular.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.060
We got on like a house on fire.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.020
We spent months corresponding over email
and chatting over the phone.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.620
And then the day finally came
when we would meet face to face.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.340
So I walk into the meeting room,

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.700
and there she is.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:41.980
I say hello,

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.220
and she kind of gives me this odd look.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.140
And she says,

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.100
"Oh, you must work for Mariam."

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.540
(Audience murmurs)

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.420
And I'm thinking, "What does she mean?"

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.220
And then it dawns on me.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.500
She must think

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.580
I'm the legal secretary
or the assistant perhaps.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.700
Not that there's anything
wrong with having those jobs.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.460
But it seems that in her mind,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.140
the voice that she's heard
on the phone all these months

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.740
doesn't seem to correspond

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.860
to the person standing in front of her.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.500
Rather unconvincingly
at that point, I say,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.980
"No, I am Mariam."

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.620
And then I start to doubt myself.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:31.540
And all those months of rapport building
just goes down the drain at that point

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:36.220
as I start again from scratch
to win her over.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.940
I do, of course, eventually win her over,

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:44.340
but it was a lot of time and effort wasted

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.900
and ultimately, yet another hit
to my confidence.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:50.220
And the thing is,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.820
I have other experiences like that one.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.020
And I know plenty
of other folks who do too.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:01.260
So today I want to reflect
on how stereotyping,

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:06.620
unconscious bias and societal privilege
operates in such a way

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.580
that creates an unequal playing field
for diverse minority groups.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.380
Now, let us start with privilege.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.700
Privilege is basically unearned advantage.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:21.020
Hey, what the hell, we really
didn't do much to earn it.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.380
It's basically access to
or enjoying rights

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.660
because you belong
to a particular identity.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.180
You're part of a club.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.500
And here's the thing.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.100
I am owning up to mine.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.460
I am privileged.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.620
Hey, and if you could afford
the ticket price,

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:38.860
you probably are too.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.340
(Laughter)

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.060
And here's the thing.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.500
Privilege is relative,
and it’s ultimately subjective.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.260
And those who have the most amounts of it,

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.860
they’re probably not
entirely conscious of it.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.140
Because for the most part,

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.180
and as they say, privilege is largely
invisible to those who have it.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.700
Now, I work as a diversity
and inclusion consultant,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.460
so I do spend a hell of a lot of my day
thinking about these concepts.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.740
Think about this.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.580
Whose faces do you see
reflected back at you

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.300
on your television screens?

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.980
Who holds the top positions
in the top ranks of society?

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.980
Now, except for the few
examples here or there,

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.100
notice the general lack
of cultural diversity?

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.620
Now, of course,

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.340
the photos that you're seeing up there
are some of our top CEOs,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.580
chancellors of universities,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.100
federal cabinet and TV
personalities in particular.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.700
Now, what we're actually
exposed to every day

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.780
feeds the assumptions
that we make about people.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.380
We all tend to possess
a strong tendency towards people

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.380
who physically resemble ourselves.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.220
It's called affinity bias.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.220
Now experiments have shown us

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:02.780
that the brain actually categorizes
by race in less than 100 milliseconds.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:08.660
Now, according to Nobel Peace Prize
recipient neuroscientist Eric Kandel,

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.300
he argues that up to 90 percent
of the brain's behavior

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.780
is actually unconscious.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:16.020
So these little shortcuts
that the brain makes,

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.820
these little implicit
associations, these biases,

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.300
for the most part,
they are done unconsciously.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.260
And here's the thing.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.180
Inherent societal privilege

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.220
means that some of us
are given a head start

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.940
in what I like to call the race of life.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.260
And it's because of that head start,

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.780
not just because we worked
extra, extra hard,

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.140
that they'll always be miles ahead,

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:47.300
even if all the participants
are running at the same speed

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.820
and with the same ability.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:52.380
So that's code for the same
amount to merits,

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.420
or in this case,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.780
running the same distance.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.380
Now, it's well documented

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.380
that women of minority groups
face even greater hurdles,

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:04.980
as you can see.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.940
They face both social and institutional
barriers to full equality,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.900
not just because they're chicks,

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:15.900
but because of things such as race,
religion, sexuality or disability.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.700
They basically have a double whammy.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:23.700
And that concept is referred to
as intersectionality.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:30.100
Now, as an Australian woman
who's originally from Afghanistan

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.660
and a Muslim,

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.300
I get the triple whammy.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.460
I get sexism, I get racism,
and I get Islamophobia.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.700
You know, some people boast
about having a fan base.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:45.660
Well, ladies and gentlemen,
I have a troll base.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:46.980
(Laughter)

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.140
Such a clever bunch,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.860
they've totally mastered the art
of intersectional insults.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.180
That's not easy to do.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.860
Now, if only I had a dollar
for every time someone said,

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.460
"But all appointment should be on merit."

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.060
Well, of course they should be.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.540
But here's the thing.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.500
Are we assuming
that the existing status quo

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:11.900
is somehow based entirely on merit?

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.900
I'm not convinced that it is.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.500
But hey, don't take my word for it.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.500
Plenty of other people think that, too.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:24.620
Now, I recall a conversation
I was having with two senior managers.

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:27.060
It was about corporate Australia’s shift

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.020
to start focusing on cultural diversity
within its leadership ranks,

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.980
now that we're doing better
around gender diversity.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.220
One of them was a woman
of Asian-Australian heritage

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.220
and she was a former colleague of mine.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:43.700
The other, a colleague of hers,
a man of Anglo-Celtic origin.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:47.660
Now it's in the context of this discussion
in which he turns to her,

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:50.420
the Asian-Australian woman, and says,

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:51.740
"There you go.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.420
Another leg up for people like you."

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.020
Now she's shocked
because they're colleagues,

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:00.340
so she says nothing.

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:02.020
But I can't stay silent.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.100
(Laughter)

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:05.220
I literally put hand out like this.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.620
(Laughter)

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.140
Got up from my pedestal and I said,

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:13.540
"When you extend a hand
to a group of people,

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:17.940
who, for far too long, have been
effectively walking in the gutter

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:21.660
while others comfortably
stroll the streets,

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:23.380
that ain't no leg up.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.900
That certainly is not a handout,

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.740
that's simply leveling the playing field."

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.300
Now, studies have shown us

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.820
that to simply obtain
the same number of interviews

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.900
as someone with an Anglo-Saxon
sounding name,

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:43.580
candidates from Indigenous, Middle Eastern
and Asian-sounding names

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.180
have to submit a ridiculous amount
of more applications.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:51.580
So in this case, when compared
to Lisa and Andrew,

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.900
and these are the real names
used in the study,

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:58.980
Nadine and Hassan have to submit
64 percent more applications,

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:03.900
while Ming and Hong have to submit
68 percent more applications.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:07.700
Guys, we're be talking about getting
a callback to secure an interview.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.820
We're not even talking
about the kind of biases that will kick in

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.220
once you're actually
at the interviewing table.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:17.860
Now, I can't tell you the number
of crappy dad jokes I've had to make

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:20.780
to make interviewers feel comfortable
when they first meet me

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.300
early on in my career.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:24.100
Thanks for laughing.

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:26.620
(Laughter)

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:29.940
Now, despite the fact
that up to 40 percent of Australians

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:32.180
have at least one parent born overseas,

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.060
no doubt that's probably
true of this arena,

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:41.140
that general diversity is not reflected
in the top ranks of society.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:45.340
Now, of course, I'm not suggesting
there's anything wrong with this,

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:49.460
assuming it is truly based
on a meritocracy.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.580
And for culturally diverse women like me,

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:53.700
well, you know that glass ceiling?

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.580
It's double glazed for people like me.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.460
And that's why I've got heels like this,
to be able to crack through it.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.780
Only a tiny percentage of ASX directors
are culturally diverse women.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:09.020
At a CEO level, it is even worse.

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:13.020
So think about all those stats
that I've been telling you about,

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.100
all those biases that we know about.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.580
What message does this send

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:25.340
to a generation of top HSC graduates
who are culturally diverse?

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.660
I'm hoping that by this point,

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:31.900
some of you are convinced
on the moral case

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:35.500
for why change is required
to level that damn playing field.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.500
But then there'll be others
who won't be convinced

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:40.380
unless we show them the dollar figures.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:42.340
Hey, don't worry, I've got it covered.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.820
There is a thing
called the diversity dividend,

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:49.260
that gender diverse companies
are 15 percent more likely

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.740
to outperform their industry average,

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:53.540
while culturally diverse companies,

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:57.980
they're 35 percent more likely
to outperform theirs.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:01.900
So diversity brings with it
a competitive advantage.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:05.220
And here's the thing,
anyone at this point who's thinking,

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:09.220
"Oh, she means tokenistic,
visible diversity."

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:10.540
No.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.580
I'm not asking you to whack on photos

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:17.940
of people that look like me
onto your website and tick a box.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:19.180
(Laughter)

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:20.580
Although that might be nice.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:23.620
(Laughter)

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:25.660
No, that's not what I'm talking about.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:29.540
Real diversity brings about
diversity of perspective,

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.780
diversity of views
and diversity of thought.

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.860
Ultimately, and of course,
the inevitable flow-on effect

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.100
is that Australian businesses
will be able to better reflect

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:41.420
and understand the needs
of a multicultural Australia

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:43.980
who is ultimately their customer base.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:46.460
So with everything I've said,

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.180
I want us to pause at this point.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.500
And I'm going to ask each of you
to please stand.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:53.420
Yes, please stand.

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:54.820
If you can't stand,

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:59.300
then I'll ask you to please
raise one hand instead.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.140
And you get to stretch your legs.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.100
No, we're not doing a yoga lesson.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:06.100
OK.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.620
I'm going to ask you
a series of questions.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:12.380
If the answer to the question is yes,

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:14.820
I want you to remain standing
or with your hand up.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.300
If the answer to the question is no,

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:18.820
then I want you to take a seat

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:20.260
and the rest of the questions

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:23.060
will be directed at those people
who are still standing.

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:27.180
Please be as honest as you can.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:29.260
Question one.

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.220
Did you have a job during high school?

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:34.820
If yes, keep standing,

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:36.780
if no, please take your seat.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:45.060
I quite like the power of ordering
all 4,000 of you to do things.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:46.220
(Laughter)

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:47.420
This is cool.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:49.900
Question two.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:53.220
Have you been discriminated against,

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:58.020
vilified or abused because of something
you cannot change about yourself?

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:04.300
And let's watch the room
as we start to see people sit.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:08.180
Question three.

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:10.020
At some point in your life,

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:14.260
you have felt like you've been
one of the few or the only persons

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.660
of your gender, race, faith,

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:19.060
sexual orientation or disability

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:22.420
in a room, in an educational
or professional setting?

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:28.700
And final question.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:35.900
Have you attended a public school
for the majority of your education?

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:38.260
Whoa.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:39.420
(Laughter)

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:41.820
Knew that would get a few of us.

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:44.740
OK.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.100
Thank you for being brave enough
to participate by the way.

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:51.060
If I answered those questions,

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:55.220
I would be standing at this point
with those of you that are still standing

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:56.500
in the arena.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:00.060
I want us to reflect
on our own levels of privilege

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:01.940
and what that means for each of us.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.940
I want you to reflect
on who's standing and who's sitting

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:07.300
without making anyone
feeling uncomfortable.

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:09.660
But let's think about that.

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:12.180
Thank you for being brave
and please take a seat now.

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:16.460
(Applause)

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:17.660
Thank you.

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.420
I want to tell you this.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:25.340
There's never been a more important time

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:29.900
for us to be conscious of our unconscious
biases and societal privileges.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:34.780
You know, when you're driving and you know
you've got a blind spot back here,

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:36.820
you know it exists, but you can't see it.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:40.420
But you make a concerted effort
to turn your head each and every time

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:42.700
to look and to adjust.

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:46.660
It's not enough to know
that we've got these biases

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.820
and that we have
these levels of privilege.

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:50.700
You've got to do something about it.

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:53.900
We're living in an era
where divisive politics

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:57.500
has successfully otherized
diverse minority groups

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:00.020
and normalized xenophobia against them.

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.620
We're constantly reading
derogatory headlines

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:04.500
about diverse minority groups.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:09.220
And what's worse is some of these remarks
are made by our elected officials.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.140
Now, I believe that when you
excuse bigotry in words,

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:18.060
you lay the framework
to give bigotry in action a free pass.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:23.740
We know that we have an urgent issue
of disenfranchisement amongst the youth,

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:26.100
particularly those of diverse backgrounds.

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:29.300
We want them to reach for the stars.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:34.620
But the thing is, you can't aspire
to be someone that you can't see.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:39.460
You can't address inequality equally.

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:40.780
You can't.

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:44.580
This is why I believe targets
are required across industries

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:47.220
where diversity of this kind is lacking.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:51.500
Now, why, you ask, why targets?

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:54.140
Because the definition of insanity

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:57.020
is doing the same thing
over and over again

00:14:57.020 --> 00:14:59.220
and expecting different results.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:02.260
(Applause)

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:03.460
Thank you.

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:05.780
(Applause)

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:10.380
The existing status quo has not allowed us
to reach a true meritocracy.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.940
So we need additional measures
to give us a bit of a nudge.

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:16.900
Because let’s face it:

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:20.460
what gets measured ultimately gets done.

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:22.580
Thank you very much, ladies and gentlemen.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:25.300
(Applause)

